= Solution
{parent=i}

Vary the gravitational volume factor and use part (a)(ii):
$$
\delta(f(R)d\operatorname{vol}_g)
=\left[f'(R)\delta R+\frac12f(R)g^{ab}h_{ab}\right]d\operatorname{vol}_g.
$$
Twice applying <integration by parts> moves both derivatives in $\delta R$ from the compactly supported $h_{ab}$ onto $f'(R)$. Combining the result with the stated matter variation and requiring every coefficient of $h_{ab}$ to vanish gives the <f(R) gravity> equation
$$
\boxed{f'(R)R_{ab}-\frac12g_{ab}f(R)
+(g_{ab}\nabla_c\nabla^c-\nabla_a\nabla_b)f'(R)
=8\pi T_{ab}}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\alpha'=1,
\qquad\beta'=-1}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For <Minkowski spacetime>, $R_{ab}=0$ and $R=0$, while $f'(R)$ is constant. In vacuum $T_{ab}=0$, so every derivative term vanishes and the remaining term is $-\tfrac12\eta_{ab}f(0)=0$. Hence Minkowski spacetime is a vacuum solution of this <modified gravity> theory.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Because $R=O(\epsilon)$ and
$$
f(0)=0,
\qquad f'(0)=1,
\qquad f''(0)=0,
$$
the <Taylor series> gives $f(R)=R+O(\epsilon^3)$ and $f'(R)=1+O(\epsilon^2)$. The extra derivative terms in the f(R) equation are therefore at least second order. At first order the theory reduces to the <linearized Einstein equations>
$$
G^{(1)}_{\mu\nu}=8\pi\mathcal T_{\mu\nu}.
$$
In <harmonic coordinates>, equivalently the <Lorenz gauge in linearized gravity> $\partial_\mu\bar h^\mu{}_\nu=0$, the <trace-reversed metric perturbation>
$$
\bar h_{\mu\nu}=h_{\mu\nu}-\frac12\eta_{\mu\nu}h
$$
satisfies
$$
\boxed{\partial^\rho\partial_\rho\bar h_{\mu\nu}
=-16\pi\mathcal T_{\mu\nu}},
\qquad
\boxed{\partial_\mu\bar h^\mu{}_\nu=0}.
$$
Thus <linearized f(R) gravity about Minkowski spacetime> has the same weak-field predictions as general relativity under these assumptions. Differences can first appear through nonlinear curvature terms or in backgrounds about which $f''(R)$ is nonzero.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

